{{!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  components/img.hbs — one responsive image, everywhere.

  Ghost generates the sizes declared in package.json → config.image_sizes
  (xxs 30 · xs 100 · s 300 · m 600 · l 1000 · xl 2000) at upload time. They
  cost nothing extra to serve and the theme was asking for exactly one of them
  per <img> — so a phone on a slow connection downloaded a 1000px cover to
  paint it 340px wide. This partial hands the browser the whole set and lets
  it choose.

  WHY A PLAIN <img> AND NOT <picture> + WebP. A <picture> wrapper would be the
  obvious next step, and it is the wrong one here: the design system styles
  media with DIRECT-CHILD selectors — `.ns-chero__media > img`,
  `.ns-card__media > :where(img, video)`, `.ns-media > img` — and inserting
  <picture> between them silently drops every one of those rules. The gain
  would be a format; the cost would be the object-fit, the aspect ratio and
  the hover scale on every card on the site. Ghost already serves modern
  formats where the origin supports it, so this is not the place to force it.

  PARAMS
    src            required. A Ghost image field: feature_image, profile_image…
    alt            "" for decoration. Passing nothing means decorative.
    sizes          the CSS box the image will occupy, so the browser can pick
                   BEFORE layout. Default assumes a full-width block; pass a
                   real value for anything in a grid or a rail — a wrong
                   `sizes` is worse than none, because it picks confidently.
                   The `src` fallback is always the 600px cut, for the handful
                   of clients that ignore srcset entirely.
    class          classes on the <img>.
    width/height   intrinsic box. Pass them wherever you know them: they are
                   what stop the page reflowing as images arrive (CLS).
    eager          true for the ONE above-the-fold image on the page — it also
                   sets fetchpriority="high". Everything else stays lazy.

  Above the fold, `loading="lazy"` is a pessimisation: the browser defers the
  very image the reader is waiting for. Exactly one image per page should pass
  `eager=true`, and it should be the LCP element.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{#if src}}
<img
  class="{{class}}"
  src="{{img_url src size="m"}}"
  srcset="{{img_url src size="s"}} 300w,
          {{img_url src size="m"}} 600w,
          {{img_url src size="l"}} 1000w,
          {{img_url src size="xl"}} 2000w"
  sizes="{{#if sizes}}{{sizes}}{{else}}100vw{{/if}}"
  alt="{{alt}}"
  {{#if width}}width="{{width}}"{{/if}}
  {{#if height}}height="{{height}}"{{/if}}
  {{#if eager}}fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"{{else}}loading="lazy" decoding="async"{{/if}}>
{{/if}}
